HISTORICAL INITIAL TEMPLATE/LAYOUT REFERENCE
This document preserves the original development table and its pinned hashes.
Its old hosted arena/output sizes are not the current compiler contract.
Use compiler.zero, LANGUAGE.ztext and selfhost/CORE.ztext for current capacities,
Windows/guest targets and later native-template overrides.

ZERO X64 CODE GENERATION DATA

This file describes our own x64 instruction encoding tables. The active source
fragment is x64-tables.zero.part, written in Zero. It was transferred read-only
from the existing Zero compiler's compiled template data. No assembler or linker
was invoked to produce this fragment or its validation programs.

TABLE CONTRACT
The caller binds @templates to a zeroed bank of 128 entries of 16 bytes.
entry + 0: u64 pointer to lowercase ASCII hexadecimal digits.
entry + 8: u64 decoded byte count.
Each pair of hexadecimal digits encodes one output byte. Strings are terminated
by NUL but the decoder uses the supplied length. Unsupported entries stay zero;
reject attempts to emit them. The longest string is 1024 characters.
IDs 0..47 are shared across backends. IDs 48..69 belong to the IA32/real extension
set. ID70 is the x64 hosted prologue with a 1 MiB reserve arena.

ID NAME                 DECODED_BYTES
 0 prologue             38
 1 entry_arguments      6
 2 immediate            2
 3 load_local           3
 4 store_local          3
 5 push_rax             1
 6 pop_rcx              1
 7 add_code             3
 8 sub_code             6
 9 mul_code             4
10 neg_code             3
11 compare_code         3
12 set_eq               3
13 set_ne               3
14 set_lt               3
15 set_gt               3
16 set_le               3
17 set_ge               3
18 normalize            3
19 test_jump            5
20 jump_code            1
21 string_address       3
22 load8_code           3
23 load16_code          3
24 load32_code          2
25 load64_code          3
26 store8_code          2
27 store16_code         3
28 store32_code         2
29 store64_code         3
30 in8_code             5
31 in16_code            6
32 out8_code            3
33 out16_code           4
34 reserve_code         40
35 argument0_code       3
36 argument1_code       3
37 foreign_call         30
38 enter_code           13
39 disable_code         1
40 halt_code            4
41 host_read_code       12
42 host_write_code      18
43 put_code             49
44 exit_code            10
45 default_exit         9
46 elf_header           120
47 pe_header            512
70 host_prologue        38

LAYOUT AND PATCH RULES
All scalar patches below are little endian. Offsets refer to the beginning of
the output unless explicitly described as relative to a template. Check output
capacity before each emit and each finalization extension.

Hosted ELF: emit ID46, then ID70 for the self-hosted compiler, then program code,
then ID45. ELF header length is 120. Entry is 0x400078. Final output length is
stored as u64 at offsets 96 and 104. ID0 retains the original 64 KiB arena for
compatibility when explicitly desired; hosted self-compilation needs ID70.

Raw x64: emit ID1, ID0, program code, ID40. There is no file header.
UEFI x64: emit ID47, ID1, ID0, program code, ID40, then finalize PE as below.
ID1 saves initial RCX/RDX into R14/R15. ID35/ID36 expose these saved arguments.

ID0 and ID70 differ only in their four-byte arena-size immediate at template
offset31. ID0 has 00 00 01 00 (65536), ID70 has 00 00 10 00 (1048576).
The offset is151 in an ordinary seed-generated hosted ELF (120+31). The
prologue also reserves/zeroes512 bytes for64 eight-byte local variable slots.
ID34 aligns allocations to16 bytes and traps on size overflow or arena
exhaustion. Arena memory is not guaranteed zero: explicitly zero buffers that
require it.

An integer expression emits ID2 followed by its u64 value.
A variable load/store emits ID3/ID4 then signed i32 displacement -8*(slot+1).
Slots are numbered0..63. The type throughout this backend is unsigned u64,
including comparison order; arithmetic wraps modulo2^64.
Binary expressions emit left, ID5, right, ID6, then their operator template.
Comparisons emit ID11, the appropriate ID12..17, and ID18.
A conditional branch emits ID19 then an i32 displacement.
An unconditional branch emits ID20 then an i32 displacement.
For either branch displacement field, value = target - (field_offset + 4).

A string expression emits ID20, u32 byte count including NUL, decoded literal
bytes and their NUL, ID21, then i32 -(byte_count+7). The literal is skipped at
runtime and ID21 obtains its position using an instruction-relative address.

Memory loads place the address in RAX before ID22..25. Stores evaluate address,
emit ID5, evaluate value, emit ID6, then ID26..29. Port output uses the same
pattern and ID32/33; input evaluates port then ID30/31. Check target permission
before emitting privileged operations.

Hosted read/write evaluates buffer address, ID5, byte count, ID6, then ID41/42.
Return value follows the Linux x64 read/write syscall convention, including
negative error codes; callers handle interrupted and partial operations.
ID43 writes one byte with interruption retry and exits74 on output failure.
ID44 consumes an exit code in RAX. ID45 exits0.
Raw/UEFI targets must reject hosted read/write.

Foreign call evaluates six expressions, emitting ID5 after each: function,
arg1, arg2, arg3, arg4, arg5. ID37 uses the x64 firmware calling convention,
including stack alignment and shadow space. Its result is in RAX.
Enter evaluates entry point, arg0, stack top, with ID5 after each, then ID38;
this disables interrupts, switches stack and jumps. It does not return.

PE32+ FINALIZATION
All fields here are u32; header size512, section alignment4096, file alignment512.
code_length = current_output_length - 512
store at336: code_length
raw_length = ceil(code_length / 512) * 512
store at92 and344: raw_length
reloc_file = 512 + raw_length
store at388: reloc_file
reloc_rva = 4096 + ceil(code_length / 4096) * 4096
store at380 and240: reloc_rva
store at144: reloc_rva + 4096
Check reloc_file + 512 <= output_capacity before adding final padding.
Zero bytes from current_output_length through reloc_file + 511.
Store u32 4096 at reloc_file and u32 12 at reloc_file+4.
Set final output length to reloc_file+512.
The relocation block contains no relocation entries; current generated code
is position independent. Directory size at244 remains12 from the header.

VERIFICATION
A temporary Zero program initialized this exact table fragment, decoded all
49 populated entries in numeric ID order and wrote1004 bytes. It was compiled
with the existing own build/zero --host and executed successfully. Its bytes
matched all extracted own templates, including the deliberate hosted arena
change. No assembler/linker was invoked.
Decoded concatenation SHA256:
8c3e4c0725a9ed5732648ea43e3034fa512263efd4010ceea2386f5454922974

A raw/UEFI fixture was compiled with the existing own compiler:
1 => @a.
@a + 2 -> @a.
!halt.
The independently assembled sequence of the table fragments and immediate
fields matched the98-byte raw output exactly. Applying the PE rules above
matched the1536-byte EFI output exactly, including all padding/header fields.
